Git Team Workflow Guide
Title
Git Team Workflow Guide: Pull Request, Code Review, and CI/CD Integration
Description
This guide describes the use of Git at the team level, focusing on collaboration through pull requests, code review, and integration with CI/CD systems to ensure project development is standardized, transparent, and easily traceable.

Scope of Use
Pull Request (PR)


Used to propose changes from a sub-branch to the main branch.
This marks the beginning of collaboration and code review.
Code Review


The team reviewed the code proposed in the PR.
Reduce errors, improve quality, and build mutual understanding.
CI/CD Integration


Use GitHub Actions or GitLab CI to inspect and automate deployments.
It streamlines the workflow and reduces manual work.

Important commands and procedures (Team Workflow Commands)
# 1. Create a branch for a new task.
git checkout -b feature/streaming-module

#2. Get to work and commit.
git add .
git commit -m "Add streaming module feature"

# 3. Push branch to remote.
git push origin feature/streaming-module

# 4. Create a Pull Request (via GitHub/GitLab UI)
# - Select the branch you want to merge into main.
# - Enter details of the change.

# 5. Code Review
# - Reviewers check the code and suggest fixes.
# - Use comments in PR to communicate.

#6. Merge PR
git checkout main
git pull
git merge feature/streaming-module
git push origin main

# 7. CI/CD Integration (GitHub Actions Example)
# - When a push or merge occurs into the main group.
# - The system will run tests and deploy automatically.


Checklist for Team Workflow
[ ] Create a new branch for each feature.
[ ] Commit with a clear message.
[ ] Push branch and create a Pull Request
[ ] Reviewer checks the code and provides feedback.
[ ] Merge PR after approval.
[ ] CI/CD pipeline runs automatically (test + deploy)
[ ] Sync Docs/Wiki after major changes.

⚖️ Principles of Use
Always use a Pull Request before merging into the main branch.
Reviewers must examine the code transparently and provide constructive feedback.
Use CI/CD to reduce errors from manual deployment.
Maintain a clear and consistent commit message and PR description.
Respect ethics and responsibility in collaborative work.

